const faqs=[
 {question:"How do you test willingness to pay before building?",answer:"Start with evidence of the problem, current alternatives, costs and budget ownership. Present a specific offer to a relevant buyer and ask for a realistic next commitment. Depending on the stage, this may be a paid pilot, deposit, pre-order, procurement review or completed purchase."},
 {question:"Can customer interviews prove willingness to pay?",answer:"Interviews can reveal current spending, economic consequences, budget processes, price expectations and barriers. They provide important evidence, but a hypothetical answer is weaker than action. Stronger validation comes from a concrete offer and an observable commitment."},
 {question:"Should you ask customers how much they would pay?",answer:"You can discuss price, but do not rely on an isolated open-ended number. First establish the customer’s current situation, value, alternatives, authority and buying process. Test defined price points or offers in a context where the answer has a realistic consequence."},
 {question:"What is the strongest evidence of willingness to pay?",answer:"Completed payment is strongest. Earlier but useful evidence includes a deposit, signed paid-pilot agreement, approved budget, procurement progress or another costly commitment. The evidence should match the size and stage of the decision."},
 {question:"What if customers value the idea but will not pay?",answer:"Determine whether the problem lacks urgency, the wrong person is being asked, another budget owns the value, the offer is poorly framed, switching costs are too high or a free alternative is sufficient. Change the segment, offer or business model only when the evidence supports the change."},
 {question:"Does a free pilot validate willingness to pay?",answer:"A free pilot can test feasibility, usage and delivered value, but it does not by itself validate payment. Agree in advance what paid next step, decision criteria, buyer involvement and timetable would follow a successful pilot."}
];
